		{"id":"e3072a4964c0a96c","type":"file","file":"Do Not Publish/Drafts/Draft 2 - Sensemaking & the Superorganism.md","x":7440,"y":-300,"width":760,"height":4260},
		{"id":"fe6be7a85dad7dd2","type":"text","text":"Collective intelligence - the sensemaking elements","x":8280,"y":-220,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"398b7984ee32bc24","type":"text","text":"How do we incorporate different voices: human, machine, animal","x":8280,"y":-140,"width":250,"height":100,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"589ea05e5d6bbd64","type":"text","text":"What does it mean to have collective wisdom?","x":8280,"y":-20,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"cff42ba8d2771b1d","type":"text","text":"Sensemaking vs meaning-making vs other ways or interpreting, new metaphors","x":8280,"y":60,"width":250,"height":140,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"89c2bf198d2db6ee","type":"text","text":"Toggle between representation (the map) and interpretation (the pyramid)","x":9005,"y":210,"width":250,"height":130,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4dfe6f4755ccd28c","type":"text","text":"Collective intelligence is shared or group intelligence that emerges from the collaboration, collective efforts, and competition of many individuals and appears in consensus decision making\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collective_intelligence","x":9005,"y":350,"width":250,"height":310,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b91546390560acba","type":"text","text":"A process by which we create intelligence","x":9005,"y":140,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a85dc0b20627eaa6","type":"text","text":"'collective intelligence' can be understood as the enhanced capacity that is created when people work together, often with the help of technology.\nhttps://www.nesta.org.uk/report/future-minds-and-machines/3-what-collective-intelligence/","x":8740,"y":140,"width":250,"height":280,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"8430f40ccfe31093","type":"text","text":"Wisdom is the use of one's knowledge and experience to make good judgements. Wisdom is the interpretating and understanding of knowledge that leads to greater insight. Wisdom is a pragmatic kind of \"praxis\" where one is constantly using metacognition.\nhttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisdom","x":8740,"y":435,"width":250,"height":355,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ec94cb4372befb27","type":"text","text":"“Capacity of judging rightly in matters relating to life and conduct; soundness of judgement in the choice of means and ends”\nhttp://www.oed.com/view/Entry/229491?rskey=nYVJ81&result=1&isAdvanced=false#eid","x":9005,"y":680,"width":250,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"1e5985ea4c1ce2cb","type":"text","text":"Global brain vs global mind","x":8740,"y":60,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"355579e2e3ffe1d3","type":"text","text":"More-than-human intelligence","x":9005,"y":60,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4eec6c992cd95144","type":"text","text":"What a wise person needs to know and understand constitutes a varied list: the most important goals and values of life — the ultimate goal, if there is one; what means will reach these goals without too great a cost; what kinds of dangers threaten the achieving of these goals; how to recognize and avoid or minimize these dangers; what different types of human beings are like in their actions and motives (as this presents dangers or opportunities); what is not possible or feasible to achieve (or avoid); how to tell what is appropriate when; knowing when certain goals are sufficiently achieved; what limitations are unavoidable and how to accept them; how to improve oneself and one’s relationships with others or society; knowing what the true and unapparent value of various things is; when to take a long-term view; knowing the variety and obduracy of facts, institutions, and human nature; understanding what one’s real motives are; how to cope and deal with the major tragedies and dilemmas of life, and with the major good things too. (Nozick 1989, 269)\nhttps://practicalphilosopher.prinzing.net/what-is-wisdom-7c9fcb2a3a9c","x":9280,"y":10,"width":460,"height":570,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"67e0429f3f9402fa","type":"text","text":"![](https://cdn-ikpjohn.nitrocdn.com/DfwgkHLwYlwOPFzEQocOyyqUcjXgQwkl/assets/images/optimized/rev-df1c67d/www.i-scoop.eu/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/DIKW-through-the-eyes-of-IoT-company-AGT-as-mentioned-on-Electronics-360.gif)","x":8740,"y":900,"width":680,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"5ba5a28950e728e6","type":"text","text":"From the poem by T.S. Eliot, The Rock:\nWhere is the wisdom we have lost in knowledge?\nWhere is the knowledge we have lost in information?","x":9280,"y":600,"width":460,"height":100,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"38165b56dea6abae","type":"text","text":"![](https://cdn-ikpjohn.nitrocdn.com/DfwgkHLwYlwOPFzEQocOyyqUcjXgQwkl/assets/images/optimized/rev-df1c67d/www.i-scoop.eu/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/Epistematica-made-an-simple-SideShare-on-the-DIKW-Paradigm-Today.gif)","x":9440,"y":900,"width":660,"height":380,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"df2d49962cbccf3d","type":"text","text":"![](https://cdn-ikpjohn.nitrocdn.com/DfwgkHLwYlwOPFzEQocOyyqUcjXgQwkl/assets/images/optimized/rev-df1c67d/www.i-scoop.eu/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/More-DIKW-infographic-on-big-data-and-DIKW-via-Doclens.gif)","x":8740,"y":1220,"width":660,"height":480,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"2081343cd8c94a9d","type":"text","text":"Wisdom here is actionable intelligence","x":9150,"y":1670,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b37b87ee61d7ba98","type":"text","text":"AI is good at complicated problems, complexity requires changing narratives","x":9275,"y":720,"width":465,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"065d74b7caee82ef","type":"text","text":"\n\n![](https://www.i-scoop.eu/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/The-traditional-data-information-knowledge-wisdom-pyramid-source-Mushon.gif)\n\nAs we process information, we move up what's known as the knowledge pyramid. At the base of this pyramid is data – raw facts and figures. As we begin to define the meaning of this data, it becomes information. When we apply this information to a context, we develop knowledge. At the top of the pyramid sits wisdom – the best way to use that knowledge to move forward. \n\nYou need wisdom in order to inform action: wisdom is not putting a tomato in a fruit salad, but knowing the right thing to do given the information and context. This progression from data to wisdom mirrors our journey in sensemaking, from raw perception to deep understanding.\n\nWhen you look at a tree, you don't just see a collection of shapes and colors, or even a 3 dimensional object. You see a tree. You are embedding the image of swaying green shapes with meaning based on your preexisting mental models, cultural background, and personal experiences. We're not born knowing what a tree is, we learn it. We then apply that knowledge to make sense of what we see around us. If it looks like a tree and quacks like a tree, it's probably a tree.\n\nThis is why two people can look at the same information and come to different conclusions – their contextual frameworks are different...\n\nWe experience the world through maps and metaphors...\n\nMetaphors serve as powerful tools for bridging the gap between the known and the unknown. They allow us to take familiar concepts and use them to understand new, complex ideas. For instance, describing the brain as a computer helps us understand certain aspects of cognition, even if the metaphor isn't perfect.\n\n==About creating maps of the territory. you have to do this because you can never know everything, the world is too complex you have to create a representation that is useful. How this explanation of sensemaking is just a map of the territory, not the territory. ==\n\nWorldbuilding process: we create the world by seeing it. Its not necessarily about how accurate to reality it is but about how well it helps us understand the world (like metaphors: low accuracy high understanding)\n\n==What does this imply about sensemaking for action?==\n==Something about cybernetics that leads into the next section.==","x":6780,"y":480,"width":580,"height":1000},
		{"id":"55ba38858231b79b","type":"text","text":"The process of moving from sensemaking to action can be conceptualized using the OODA loop, developed by military strategist John Boyd. OODA stands for Observe, Orient, Decide, and Act. In this model:\n\n- Observe: We gather raw data from our environment.\n- Orient: We make sense of this data, fitting it into our existing mental models.\n- Decide: Based on our sensemaking, we choose a course of action.\n- Act: We implement our decision.\n\nThis model is not perfect, but it's useful in understanding how sensemaking connects to action. Data must be interpreted in order to make desicions to guide our actions. Each action we take provides new observations, which feed back into our sensemaking process, continually refining our understanding and subsequent actions.","x":6780,"y":5,"width":580,"height":415},
		{"id":"ae45645c04fabee3","type":"text","text":"Start talking about sensemaking between people. What does it look like? Could be just a simple dialogue. Dilogue is the basic form of sensemaking in a group. \n\nPotentially discuss a romantic couple. Creating the world with the other, when they break up want to talk about what heppened with the other person. Connect to the eyes.\n\nWhat is a superorganism? a group of individuals acting as a single organism.\n\n> *“Imagine a system with clearly defined goals, sensors and effectors for reading and interacting with the world, the ability to recognize pleasure and pain as attractors and things to avoid, the resources to carry out its will, and the legal and social standing to see its needs are catered for, even respected. That’s a description of an AI – It’s also a description of a modern corporation. For this ‘corporate AI’, pleasure is growth and profitability, and pain is lawsuits and drops in shareholder value. Corporate speech is protected, corporate personhood recognized, and corporate desires are given freedom, legitimacy and sometimes violent force by international trade laws, state regulation – or lack thereof – and the norms and expectations of a capitalist society… Crucially, they lack empathy, or loyalty, and they are hard – although not impossible – to kill.”*\n> James Bridle, [[Ways of Being - Animals, Plants, Machines]]\n\nHow do they do this? data gathering, north starts, strategy planning etc – sensemaking processes\nPsilocybernetic orgs\nNation states are also superorganisms or \"nation AI\" similar to corporate AI\nDemocracy as a sensemaking process – Choosing one image rather than putting them together\n\nThe questions is: how do we create shared maps of reality that help us take action.\n\nrole of the media\nrole of social media: dissolution of parallax: can't put the images together when no shared reality\nBig data: does it equal big knowledge or wisdom?\n\nWarm data labs – warm vs cold data\nDemocratic frame of mind\nThe role of values: Joe Edelman, that article about underlying values in discussions\n\nWhat's the role of what we call AI in all of this?\nWhat are humans good at? Contextualizing, meaning-making\n\nMaybe sensemaking is not just for action, but it defo HAS to come before action.","x":6780,"y":1500,"width":580,"height":1240},
