		{"id":"0c2804082ba0437b","type":"text","text":"fractured meaning-making","x":8570,"y":2155,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"f4d57e56aa5f61fd","type":"text","text":"Sensemaking is \n# 1+1=3 \n\nSomething new is created in the process, so the outcome is more than the sum of its parts.","x":7110,"y":-300,"width":250,"height":270},
		{"id":"47040cf856b64dcb","type":"text","text":"Incorporating different kinds of voices: human, machine, more-than-human\n\nPerhaps even more challenging is the task of incorporating more-than-human voices into our collective intelligence. This means developing ways to understand and factor in the needs and \"perspectives\" of ecosystems, non-human species, and the planet as a whole. Indigenous cultures often have sophisticated ways of considering the more-than-human world in their decision-making processes, and we would do well to learn from these traditions as we develop our global sensemaking capabilities.\n\nAs we face increasingly complex global challenges, there's a growing recognition of the need for collective wisdom. This distinction is crucial. While collective intelligence might allow us to process vast amounts of information quickly, collective wisdom is about making good decisions that consider long-term consequences and ethical implications.\n\nWhat is wisdom?\n\nGlobal brain vs global mind: not just the hardware, beyond the brain","x":6780,"y":2800,"width":580,"height":520},
		{"id":"1632721fc425c2ce","type":"text","text":"narrative building","x":8570,"y":1840,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"2dcf34400ffc44fa","type":"text","text":"There are actually at least four distinct types of meaning-making that we do all the time:\n\nType 1: Deciding that something is subjectively good or bad. “Diamonds are beautiful,” or “blood diamonds are morally reprehensible.”\n\nType 2: Deciding that something is subjectively worth doing (or not). “Going to college is worth the tuition,” or “I want to hang out with Bob, but it’s too much trouble to go all the way to East London to meet him.”\n\nType 3: Deciding what the subjective value-orderings and degrees of commensuration of a set of things should be. “Howard Hodgkin is a better painter than Damien Hirst, but Hodgkin is not as good as Vermeer,” or “I’d rather have a bottle of Richard Leroy’s ‘Les Rouliers’ in a mediocre vintage than six bottles of Vieux Telegraphe in a great vintage.”\n\nType 4: Deciding to reject existing decisions about subjective quality/worth/value-ordering/value-commensuration. “I used to think the pizza at this restaurant was excellent, but after eating at Pizza Dada, I now think it is pretty mid,” or “Lots of eminent biologists believe that kin selection theory explains eusociality, but I think they are wrong and that group selection makes more sense.”\n\nMeaning-making is about subjective choice, which is why it underlies judgment, aesthetics, taste, and fashion.\n\nThe human ability to make meaning is inherently connected to our ability to be partisan or arbitrary, to not follow instructions precisely, to be slipshod — but also to do new things, to create stuff, to be unexpected, to not take things for granted, to reason.\n\n[AI's meaning-making problem.](https://uncertaintymindset.substack.com/p/ai-meaningmaking)","x":8240,"y":3320,"width":540,"height":860,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"f43748c9f4825501","type":"text","text":"### Data management\nAI systems become relatively better than humans for data management (storage, search, and retrieval) the larger the dataset size (amount of data stored) and complicatedness (number of types of data stored) — but only where the data to be managed is both explicatable and already explicated.\n\nWhere important parts of the content to be managed are still tacit and not yet explicated, using AI systems for data management leads to lost information — information that is valuable for analysis which hasn’t been captured digitally or is unfindable and unretrievable\n### Data analysis\nAI systems become relatively better than humans for data analysis as datasets get larger and more complicated, and as patterns in the datasets become weaker or noisier — but only where a) patternings are pre-established to be meaningful by humans and/or b) the analyses are designed to highlight _**potentially**_ **meaningful patterns for human meaning-making.**\n\nIf AI systems for data analysis don’t work to patterns previously established to be meaningful by humans, the results of their analysis are potentially and unpredictably flawed.\n### Rule-following\nAI systems become relatively better than humans at rule-following as the rules become more complicated and/or tedious, and the more fully specifiable and fully specified the rules — but only if they are designed to explicitly surface unanticipated edge-cases for human meaning-making.\n\nWe rely on (ideally well-trained human) discretion when we don’t know yet how to write fully specified rules. This can happen when we’re doing innovation work (i.e., learning how to do something new, where neither the process nor the definition of a successful outcome are known in advance) and when we confront unpredictably changing environments.\n\n[Where AI wins.](https://uncertaintymindset.substack.com/p/where-ai-wins)","x":8800,"y":3320,"width":540,"height":1100,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a0ac114086d99494","type":"text","text":"Wisdom is knowledge of how to live well, and the disposition to act appropriately on that knowledge... Being wise, therefore, means knowing what leads to or constitutes human wellbeing, and being motivated to pursue it.\n[What is Wisdom?](https://practicalphilosopher.prinzing.net/what-is-wisdom-7c9fcb2a3a9c)","x":8240,"y":3140,"width":540,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3a0961d5c045f01f","type":"text","text":"The Deep Rationality Theory (DRT):\n\nThis theory proposes wisdom as a form of deep, comprehensive rationality. According to DRT, a wise person should have:\n\n1. Justified beliefs across various academic subjects: A wise person possesses well-reasoned beliefs about a wide range of topics, including science, history, philosophy, and other academic disciplines. These beliefs are not necessarily absolute knowledge, but rather well-supported understandings based on evidence and logical reasoning.\n2. Justified beliefs about rational living: This involves a deep understanding of how to live rationally in three primary domains: \n\ta) Epistemology: The wise person knows how to form and evaluate beliefs effectively, using critical thinking and evidence-based reasoning. \n\tb) Morality: They have a well-developed ethical framework and understand how to act ethically in various situations. \n\tc) Practicality: The wise individual knows how to navigate daily life efficiently and effectively, making good decisions to achieve their goals.\n1. Commitment to living rationally: Wisdom isn't just about understanding rationality; it requires a dedication to applying this understanding in one's life. A wise person consistently strives to make rational decisions and act in accordance with their well-reasoned beliefs.\n2. Few unjustified beliefs and awareness of limitations: A key aspect of wisdom is epistemic humility. The wise person is cautious about forming beliefs without sufficient evidence or reasoning. They also recognize the limits of their own knowledge and abilities, understanding that there is always more to learn and that their current beliefs may be subject to revision in light of new information.\nClaude summary based on [Wisdom (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)](https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/wisdom/)","x":8800,"y":2405,"width":540,"height":895,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"dc7127e9b3c66667","type":"text","text":"We are beginning to see human civilization as an emerging planetary superorganism ==(another layer/organ system in the gaia organism)==. ==(For the following points: upgrading not adding new, what was the circulatory system before?)== Our global trade networks act as a circulatory system, moving resources and information around the world. Our communication technologies serve as a nervous system, allowing rapid transmission of signals across vast distances. And our collective actions shape the planet's systems in ways that ==mirror the relationship between an organism and its environment (we are part of the organism not organism vs environment)==.","x":8900,"y":1920,"width":440,"height":440},
		{"id":"465c9b84ed30310e","type":"text","text":"==something about global problems being a result of bad sensemaking? as we act as a global superorganism, we need to sensemake like one.==\n\n==this brings up lots of questions about what is intelligence, reflecting James Bridle's book Ways of Being.==","x":6780,"y":3340,"width":580,"height":140},
		{"id":"c308a68c8099e834","type":"text","text":"using AI to give voice to nature: river don","x":8335,"y":2450,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
