		{"id":"b37016fafe066f69","type":"text","text":"\n\n## From Data to Action\n==DIKW pyramid? says wisdom but really action intelligence==\n\n==this kind of action intelligence might work in complicated situations where there is one right answer, but what about complex ones? ie the majority. give an example of a situation that is more complex.==\n\n==give examples of AI fuck ups like benefits in Australia==\n\n==collective intelligence vs collective wisdom. what is the difference? lets explore...==\n\n## Wisdom Through Meaning-Making","x":6780,"y":3500,"width":580,"height":780},
		{"id":"99973203b5092f8a","type":"text","text":"How do we take action collectively towards the right goal? wisdom is the goal, collective action is intelligence","x":8280,"y":2940,"width":250,"height":160,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"a37897e3ff1f1d8f","type":"text","text":"new perspectives = more wisdom","x":8550,"y":2480,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"71ceca6604b8a279","type":"text","text":"![[Pasted image 20240927133351.png]]","x":8740,"y":-659,"width":730,"height":669},
		{"id":"a803bf88e204f4d9","type":"text","text":"In the 1970s, James Lovelock proposed the Gaia hypothesis, suggesting that our planet functions as a self-regulating, complex  living organism. This revolutionary concept posits that the biosphere, atmosphere, hydrosphere, and geosphere are intricately linked, working together to maintain the conditions necessary for life.\n\n","x":8280,"y":300,"width":380,"height":240},
		{"id":"386a9ce8b5f9b7e7","type":"text","text":"strategy and resource allocation","x":8210,"y":1110,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"6"}
	],
